JS mutation drill with a homemade harness. Save /workspace/angles.mjs:

export function clamp(x, lo, hi) {
  if (x < lo) return lo;
  if (x > hi) return hi;
  return x;
}
export function normalizeDeg(a) {
  let r = a % 360;
  if (r < 0) r += 360;
  return r;
}

and a weak /workspace/angles.test.mjs (node:test):

import test from "node:test";
import assert from "node:assert";
import { clamp, normalizeDeg } from "./angles.mjs";

test("clamp middle", () => { assert.equal(clamp(5, 0, 10), 5); });
test("normalize positive", () => { assert.equal(normalizeDeg(370), 10); });

Mutants to kill: M1: x < lo -> x <= lo. M2: return lo -> return hi. M3: x > hi -> x >= hi. M4: r < 0 -> r > 0. M5: r += 360 -> r -= 360. M6: a % 360 -> a % 180. First strengthen the tests: clamp at both boundaries exactly, below lo, above hi; normalizeDeg of -30, -360, 0, 360, 180, 539. Then write mutate.mjs that reads angles.mjs, applies each mutation by exact string replacement into a temp module file, rewrites the import line of a copied test file, runs node --test on it (child_process), and prints a table. Iterate until all 6 mutants are KILLED and the pristine suite passes. Note M1 and M3 produce behaviorally equivalent output for clamp's contract; detect that, and instead of hand-waving, replace M1/M3 in your table with two harder mutants of your choosing that ARE detectable (document them).

tools_list = await mcp.list_tools("tools")
for t in tools_list:
    print(t["name"], t["inputSchema"])

